    { "answer": ["Alcohol withdrawal","Delirium tremens","DT","DTs"], "clues": ["In 'The Adventures of Huckleberry Finn', Huck's father suffers from this condition where he thought snakes were biting his legs. If that's not helpful, Nicolas Cage also played a character who  in the 1995 film, Leaving Las Vegas. His performance won him the Academy Award for Best Actor in 1996","For centuries, this was misidentified as 'phrenitis' or an inflammation of the brain. It was formally identified and named in 1813, which marked a turning point in the clinical recognition of this as a disease that could be managed medically","Patients might develop confusion, shaking, and vivid hallucinations which popularized an older idiom 'seeing pink elephants'"] }

{ "answer": ["mycophenolate","MMF","Cellcept","mycophenolate mofetil","mycophenolic acid"], "clues": ["This was first isolated in 1893 from a fungus, Penicillium brevicompactum. When culturing a fungus growing on bad corn, Bartolomeo Gosio noted that the fungus produced this active antibacterial compound. Unfortunately, this discovery was published in an obscure Italian medical journal and went unnoticed until much later and is not used as an antibiotic today","In the late 1980s, a South African geneticist was studying children with rare, severe immunodeficiencies. He noticed that when children lacked a specific enzyme to make guanosine, their T and B cells completely failed to multiply. He realized that this drug intentially mimics this exact defect.","This was approved by the FDA in 1995 and is increasingly utilized off label as a steroid sparing treatment in autoimmune diseases although was originally approved for the prevention of organ transplant rejection"] },
{ "answer": ["Blastomycosis","Blastomyces"], "clues": ["When analyzing the skeletal remains of the Lake Woodland indigenous peoples of North America, who lived roughly between 500 and 1000AD, paleopathologists noticed a stark uptick in destructive bone lesions compared to ealier eras. Researchers hypothesize that as these ancient societies shifted toward intensive agriculture, they inadvertently dug up and inhaled massive amounts of spores from this organism, contributing to shortened lifespans observed during the agricultural transition","Dogs, especially hunting, sporting, and large breeds that spend time sniffing and digging in wet soil, are up to ten times more susceptible to this fungus than humans. It is not typically contageous from animals to humans","In the chilly environment of outdoor dirt and wood, this grows as a harmless, fuzzy mold. However, once its tiny spores are inhaled into the warm human or animal body, they morph into large, multiplying yeasts that can invade tissue. Under the microscope, the yeast can be easily recognized by its unique 'broad-based buds'."] },
{ "answer": ["Multiple Myeloma","MM"], "clues": ["The most famous early story of this disease was of Mr. McBean. His physician noticed that his urine grew remarkably thick and cloudy when heated, but cleared up completely when boiled further. While this boiling test was used to diagnose patients for generations, it is no longer used today as more sensitive tests were developed","In 1992, a man named James Bond from Ohio was diagnosed with this disease. At the time, the median survival was 2-3 years. Refusing to give up, he underwent 4 separate stem cell transplants and 6 clinical trials. In 2002, he entered a clinical trial for a drug with the code name 'trial 007'. He lived for more than 29 years post-diagnosis","'Trial 007' was for bortezomib, which went on to revolutionize care of this disease globally."] },
{ "answer": ["renal carcinoma","Renal cancer","kidney cancer","Renal cell carcinoma","RCC","kidney tumor","renal tumor","kidney carcinoma","renal CA"], "clues": ["The first formal classification system for this was proposed in 1826 by König. Before modern microscopic, genetic, and molecular pathology, he divided these into scirrhous, steatomatous, fungoid, and medullary purely based on their appearance.","Because some of these contained lipid-rich cells resembling the adrenocortical zona fasciculata, in 1883, Grawitz theorized that some of these originated from displaced or ectopic adrenal tissue that became trapped in nearby structures.","Roughly 70% of cases are found incidentally on CT or ultrasound as early stages can be asymptomatic. The most common type is named for the clear or pale appearance of the cells under a microscope due to high levels of glycogen and lipids."] },
{ "answer": ["Adrenal glands","adrenals","adrenal gland"], "clues": ["A prominent Italian anatomist first documented these in 1564 but early scientists had no idea what they did. For nearly two centuries, they believed these were hollow capsules filled with black bile (capsulae atrabiliarae)","In the 1800s, scientists realized that removing these was rapidly fatal. A French physiologist Brown-Séquard became obsessed with the idea that these structures secreted a mysterious, life-extending force. He famously experimented on himself by injecting liquid extracts from animals, claiming it was an anti-aging elixir that restored his youthful vigor","Despite weighing just a fraction of an ounce, these glands control vital functions like blood pressure, metabolism, and your "fight-or-flight" response"] },
